<BaseLayout title="Timing Screws: Feed Screws, Scrolls & Infeed Screws Explained" description="How timing screws (feed screws, scrolls, infeed worms) space and time containers into fillers, cappers, and labelers. Pitch design, single vs dual, materials, drives, and troubleshooting." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/timing-screw/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
<h1>Timing Screws, Feed Screws &amp; Scrolls</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A timing screw is a rotating helical screw beside a packaging conveyor that captures each container in a pocket between its threads and releases it at a set spacing and time. Its most common job is metering round containers into the star wheel of a filler, capper, or labeler. Cut differently, the same device groups, combines, divides, turns, and inverts containers.</p>
<h2 id="what-heading">What Is a Timing Screw?</h2>
<p>A timing screw is a threaded rod that turns alongside the conveyor while containers ride on the belt. Each thread pocket holds one container (or a group), and each turn of the screw moves every container forward by one pocket. Unlike a fastening screw, its thread pitch is usually not uniform, so the screw can open gaps between touching containers as they travel along it.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/starwheel-septimatech-infeed-screw.webp" width="1000" height="666" alt="A single dark green helical timing screw running alongside a row of large filled water bottles, with each bottle sitting in a flight of the screw." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>A single infeed screw for soft-wall containers, with water bottles seated in the screw pockets. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.septimatech.com/products-and-services/machine-changeovers/feed-screws-systems/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Septimatech</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<p>The device goes by many names. <a href="https://morrison-chs.com/timing-screws/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Morrison Container Handling Solutions</a>, a timing screw manufacturer, lists "worms, feed screws, augers, scrolls, infeed screws, gusanos, and tornillos sin fin." Krones calls it the infeed worm. They all describe the same part. A timing screw is not a <a href="/conveyor-types/screw-conveyors/">screw conveyor</a>, which moves bulk material inside a trough.</p>
<h2 id="anatomy-heading">Timing Screw Anatomy</h2>
<p>The <a href="https://www.fraingroup.com/wp-content/uploads/Timing-Screws.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Frain Industries timing screw white paper</a>, written by packaging machinery author John Henry, defines the dimensions a buyer or replacement-parts supplier needs.</p>
