<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a timing screw?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A rotating helical screw beside a packaging conveyor that captures containers in the pockets between its threads and releases them at a set spacing and time. Also called a feed screw, infeed screw, scroll, worm, or helix. Its most common job is metering round containers into the star wheel of a rotary filler, capper, or labeler, but screws also transfer, group, combine, divide, turn, and invert containers.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How is timing screw pitch matched to a star wheel?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The discharge pitch must equal the star wheel pocket spacing, and the screw must release one container each time a pocket reaches the handoff. In the Frain Industries example, a screw feeding an 8-pocket star wheel turns once per one-eighth turn of the star wheel, and a 6-inch pocket spacing needs a 6-inch discharge pitch.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What are timing screws made of?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Mostly engineering plastics: UHMW polyethylene, acetal (Delrin is a common brand), nylon, or HDPE. Metal screws in stainless steel, steel, brass, or aluminum are used where cans, glass, or high speed would wear plastic quickly. Plastics can be color coded with their matching change parts. For food or drug contact, ask which FDA regulation the exact grade complies with.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">When do you need a dual timing screw?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>When one screw cannot control the container. A counter-rotating pair holds oval or non-round containers in a fixed orientation for labeling. A stacked pair supports tall, unstable containers. Pairs also combine two lanes into one, divide one lane into two, or turn oblong containers 90 degrees.</p>
</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">Why do containers tip or jam in a timing screw?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>Usually a poor infeed, a short backlog, the wrong conveyor speed, or a screw out of phase with the star wheel. Keep a backlog at the infeed, run the conveyor slightly faster than the screw so it pushes each container against the leading edge of its pocket, and use keyed or pinned mounting so the screw always goes back in time. Sharp-cornered square containers may need an inverse taper or cone infeed.</p>
</div>
